-- title:
--   Good generators of the special fibre from cusp-component points
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $M\ge 5$ is a natural number with $p\nmid M$, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$, $\zeta\in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and $K$ is the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ over $L$ given by `hK` as the base change [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb Q$. Further, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$, and $j\in K$ is a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157). The morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), written $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ below, is the two-chart model over $A$ attached to these data, glued from the spectra of the subalgebras `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` and `chartAlgInf A ↥K j`; it is assumed proper. There are fixed $A$-algebra structures on an algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of $\mathbb Q$ and on $L$, forming a scalar tower.
--
--   Special-fibre geometry: $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra; $c_1:C_1\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ and $c_2:C_2\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; $i_1,i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1,C_2$ over $k$ into the base change $X_k=$ `baseChange A (TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j) k`; `hcover` says every point of $X_k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says the fibre product $C_1\times_{X_k}C_2$ (the crossings) is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` say its number of points equals $n>0$.
--
--   Sections and Picard data: $\varepsilon$ is a section of $X$ over $A$, $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$ are $k$-sections of $c_1,c_2$, and `hε₁` says that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$. $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$, that is, a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}A$ and a zero section; `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by `algEquivZeroCut`, whose predicate on a rigidified line bundle is `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero` (a Poincaré rigidified bundle on $X\times D.\mathrm{toBase}$ satisfying the condition, universality of the classifying morphism up to isomorphism of the pulled-back bundle, and triviality along the zero section). The morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is assumed smooth (`hsm`) and separated (`hsep`). `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for $X_k$ with the base-changed designation $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k$ and the base-changed section, and `hPk` compares its Poincaré bundle with the base change along $k$ of the Poincaré bundle of $D$ pulled back along the first projection. $D_1,D_2$ are designations for $c_1,c_2$ with representability data `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. The morphism $\nu_2$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $k$ is characterised by `hν₂`: for every $k$-scheme $t:T\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and $\mathrm{pr}_2$, of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the bundle classified by $a$.
--
--   Geometric generic model: $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to the geometric generic fibre $X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}A}\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`); the preimage in $M_\eta.C$ of the finite chart `(TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j) ''ᵁ ⊤` is nonempty, and `hMηpin` pins the identification of function fields: for every $a$ in `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the germ of $a$ at the generic point, transported through $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection and through $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image under $L\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the Laurent expansion of $a$ in $K$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x,x'$ of $M_\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by the same composite for $x$, then $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x'$ is the translate of $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$ by [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54).
--
--   Special-fibre group data: $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), consisting of abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}\le G.J_{0s}$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj}:G.J_{0s}\to G.J_I\times G.J_E$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$ with the $k$-sections of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say each dictionary turns addition into the tensor product of the pulled-back Poincaré bundles, and `hproj` says that for $x\in G.J_{0s}$ the point $\mathrm{ptsI}((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_1)$ is $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ postcomposed with the Picard pullback morphism along $i_1$, while $\mathrm{ptsE}((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_2)$ is $\mathrm{pts}\,x$ postcomposed with $\nu_2$.
--
--   Generic dictionary and Hecke action: `gpts` is a bijection from $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb Q})=$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, onto the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep.some` via `algEquivZeroGroupCut` (`hgadd`). The family $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}A$; `hφmul` says each $\varphi\,t$ is additive for the relative group law on points over any base, and `hφpts` says, for the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129), that $\mathrm{gpts}(t\cdot x)$ is $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ followed by $\varphi\,t$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi block (fifteen binders, summarised here): `hDL` is representability for the base change of $X$ to $L$ with the designation $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L$, `hPL` the corresponding comparison of Poincaré bundles, $\mathrm{ajL}$ a morphism from $X_L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $L$ sending the base-changed section $\varepsilon$ to the zero section (`hajLε`) and satisfying `hajL`: for every field $K'$, every $t:\operatorname{Spec}K'\to\operatorname{Spec}L$ and every $t$-point $x$ of $X_L$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $t$ followed by the section, so $\mathrm{ajL}$ is the Abel–Jacobi map $x\mapsto[x]-[\varepsilon]$. The morphism $kL$ compares the base changes to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and to $L$ (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`), $\mathrm{ajbar}$ is defined by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by $kL$, $\mathrm{ajL}$ and the first projection, and lies over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hajbar_over`); $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point of $M_\eta.C$ inducing the base change of $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and sent by $\mathrm{ajbar}$ to the zero section (`hεbar_aj`). Finally `hpts_aj` says: for all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x,s$ of $M_\eta.C$ such that $s$ induces the base change of $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ on `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` equal to $[M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x]-[M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,s]$ with $\mathrm{gpts}(\mathrm{Pic0.mk}\,D_v)$ equal to $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$.
--
--   Igusa block: $w$ is a [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16), namely a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral power series realising its $q$-expansion and with nonzero reduction in $k$; $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ is a `CurveModel` over $k$ of the Igusa field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), $e_1$ an isomorphism $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C\cong C_1$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`), the preimage of the finite chart in $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C$ is nonempty, and `hgauss₁` is the Gauss reading: for $a$ in `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` and power series $x,y$ over $A$ with $y$ of nonzero reduction, if the Laurent expansion of $a$ times the series of $y$ over $L$ equals the series of $x$ over $L$, then the germ of $a$ transported to the Igusa field along $e_1$, $i_1$ and the first projection has Laurent expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$ over $k$. Moreover $\theta_1$ is an isomorphism of $G.J_I$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa field, pinned by `hθpin₁`: for $g\in G.J_I$ and a $k$-point $x$ of $c_1$, if the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_1$ along $\mathrm{ptsI}\,g$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon_1$, then there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to $[\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}(x\circ e_1^{-1})]-[\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}(\varepsilon_1\circ e_1^{-1})]$ with $\theta_1 g$ its class. Finally $\mathrm{frobIg}$ is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa field over $k$ acting on Laurent coefficients by $\lambda\mapsto\lambda^p$ (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   Subgroup-scheme block: $\mathcal A$ is a scheme with a morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec}A$ and a closed immersion $\iota$ into $D.P$ over $a$ (`h𝒜cl`), with $a$ proper (`h𝒜pr`) and smooth (`h𝒜sm`), all of whose fibres over algebraically closed fields are connected (`h𝒜conn`); `h𝒜grp` says the points of $\mathcal A$ form a subgroup for the relative group law (existence of the unit, closure under multiplication and under inversion after composing with $\iota$); `h𝒜gen` says that an element $x$ of `JOne (M * p)` lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) if and only if $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ factors through $\mathcal A$; and `h𝒜hecke` says $\mathcal A$ is stable under each $\varphi\,t$.
--
--   Place block: $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $p$ is a nonunit (`hPl`), $\rho:A\to\mathrm{Pl}$ is a ring homomorphism inducing the algebra map $A\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρ`), $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ contained in $\mathrm{Pl}$ with a homomorphism $\rho_O:A\to O$ inducing the same algebra map, and $\pi_k:\mathrm{Pl}\to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism with $\pi_k\circ\rho$ the algebra map $A\to k$.
--
--   Conclusion. Let $c,c'$ be $k$-points of $C_1$ (sections of $c_1$ over the identity of $\operatorname{Spec}k$) such that neither image point lies in the image of the first projection of $C_1\times_{X_k}C_2$, and such that the image of each under $i_1$ followed by the first projection $X_k\to X$ lies in the image of the finite chart `TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`. Then there exist places $P,P'$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a $\mathrm{Pl}$-point $zz$ of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ (a section over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$), an element $t\in G.J_{0s}$, a proof $hDv$ that $[P]-[P']$ has degree zero, and a proof $hdz$ that $[\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}(c\circ e_1^{-1})]-[\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}(c'\circ e_1^{-1})]$ has degree zero as a divisor on the Igusa field, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, every place $Q$ in the support of $[P]-[P']$ reduces well: there exist a $\mathrm{Pl}$-point $\xi$ of $X$ (a section over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$) and a $k$-point $d$ of $C_1$ with $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{Pl}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $\xi$ equal to the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}^{-1}(Q)$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection, with $d$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k)$ followed by $\xi$, with the image point of $d$ outside the image of the first projection of $C_1\times_{X_k}C_2$, and with the image of $d$ in $X$ inside the finite chart.
--
--   Secondly, $zz$ extends the class of $[P]-[P']$: the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}(\mathrm{Pic0.mk}\,\langle[P]-[P'],hDv\rangle)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{Pl}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $zz$.
--
--   Thirdly, $t$ is the reduction of $zz$: the point $\mathrm{pts}\,t$ followed by the first projection $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k\to D.P$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k)$ followed by $zz$.
--
--   Fourthly, $\theta_1((G.\mathrm{proj}\,t)_1)$ is the class $\mathrm{Pic0.mk}\,\langle[c]-[c'],hdz\rangle$ of the difference of the places of $c$ and $c'$ on the Igusa field, and $(G.\mathrm{proj}\,t)_2=0$.
--
--   This is a step in the description of the Néron special fibre of the Jacobian of $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$ in the Deligne–Rapoport/Raynaud picture: it produces, from a pair of $k$-points of the cusp component lying off the crossings and in the finite chart, a point of the Jacobian model over a valuation ring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ above $p$ whose generic fibre is a difference of two places with good reduction and whose reduction has Igusa component the prescribed divisor class and trivial elliptic component. It is used in the two subsequent statements that compute the Igusa component of the reduction of Hecke-translated classes in the norm-free part and that propagate the vanishing of the second component.
